A||N||6||MontanaFiero||intake gaskets-- remove pushrods?!?!||||5||
Z||000000||MontanaFiero||08-23-2001||02:17 AM||||The gaskets <I>I</I> got from carquest are the same as the gaskets that are on the motor for the lower intake manifold. While I suppose this is a good thing, it still requires me to pull the pushrods out and totally re-adjust the rockers, which is just more work than I wanted to do.  Now, the Haynes manual shows a different type of lower intake gasket that doesn't appear to require the removal of the pushrods and all that re-adjustment crap (which I've never done and don't want to f*** it up). Has anyone ever used this alternate gasket and do they know who makes it??  Before I open the package I'd like to make this job as easy as possible.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||08-23-2001||08:05 AM||||When I bought my Grooms long block I didn't  have to remove the pushrods to mount the intake manifold pieces.  How about posting a picture of the gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||08-23-2001||11:01 AM||||What is the part# that you have??<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||08-23-2001||11:12 AM||||If you didn't pull the heads off, you can count the number of turns to get cleaance to fit the pushrods back in, then put the nut right back to the same location.  I've had success with this method in the past, but I had to go through the whole lash adjustment this time, since I had the valves reseated.<BR>It takes exactly 4 complete turns to get clearance, unless the valve is open(ing).  Then it's about 6 turns, but that's about at the end of the rocker arm stud threads.  I also used liquid paper to make a reference mark on the nut/stud, to make sure it was exactly the same spot.  <BR>The GM 60 degree V-6 book says you can cut the top of the intake gasket, but Fel-Pro doesn't recommend it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MontanaFiero||08-23-2001||12:00 PM||||you don't know just how tempting that is---to cut the top of the intake gasket-- just so I don't have to go through the mess of valve adjustment. <BR><B>Can I use "form-a-gasket" to hold it in place?</B><P>&gt;&gt;&gt;wish I had a scanner so I could post a picture of the gasket that the Haynes manual is using in their intake manifold section. Maybe someone else has one?&lt;&lt;&lt;<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||08-23-2001||01:12 PM||||Most intake gaskets (Fel-Pro) have a barb fitting to hold them in place.  They also do not recommend any type of sealer except for the manifold end seals, which they provide a tube of black rtv for anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MontanaFiero||08-23-2001||01:21 PM||||thanks, that helps... off to the shop, will post photos soon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
